<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    Most people's calendar is a work calendar with some of their life
    smuggled into it. Everything that doesn't fit — the letter with a
    deadline, the renewal, the thing the school sent home — lives on the
    fridge, in an email, or in the vague hope of remembering.
  </p>
  <p>
    This one is the other way round. It's a calendar of a life, and work is
    one of the things on it.
  </p>
</section>

<%= about_screenshot("calendar") %>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What lands on it</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Things you put there") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Appointments, birthdays, the MOT. Added in a few seconds, taggable
        to as many parts of your life as they honestly belong to.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Deadlines the app found") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        When a tool drafts your appeal, the date you must respond by lands
        here by itself. The deadline that would have been lost in a letter
        becomes a thing on a calendar, which is the entire difference.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Calendars you already keep") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Existing calendars can sync in, so this doesn't demand you abandon
        what your workplace or your phone already runs.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "What others have shared") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        A partner's shared appointments appear alongside yours, which is how
        you notice that two people have promised to be in different places
        at the same school pickup.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">One thing, several places</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Your mother's cardiology appointment is a health thing and a family
    thing. Most software makes you file it as one and then lose it under the
    other. Here it carries both labels, appears under both, and is still one
    appointment rather than two copies that drift apart.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">It works with nothing configured</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    This is ordinary, sturdy calendar software that happens to sit inside an
    assistant. No model is involved in showing you next week, and none is
    required for any of it.
  </p>
</section>
